A||||7||BOILERMAKER||Punching a 2.8||||1
Z||000000||BOILERMAKER||10-21-1999||09:10 AM||||OK, not literally, but I am curious about punching a 2.8 into a 3.1.<P>I am going to do some engine modifications.  I might either get the 3300 engine or punch a 2.8 that I have down to the block in a shed.  How much can I punch before I have to worry about hitting water jackets and all of that stuff.  I was looking on the net, but couldn't find anything on this so far.<P>I know that I will have to get a different crank and cam, so I will do a search on this forum for the info (if there is any).  If anybody has any other information about this transformation, I would greatly appreciate it.<P>I know several people have already done this and I want to be part of the group.  Plus I want to do something this winter before the paintjob next summer.||||reg||1
Z||000001||BENGAL4||10-21-1999||09:31 AM||||Hey Boilermaker,<P>I have read in some posts here that fastonpumpgas is doing a 2.8 to 3.1 right now. I am purchasing an 86GT parts car this evening for the purpose of doing the same thing. He lives in southern Ohio also. Maybe the three of us could get together and swap notes. <P>Bill ||||reg||14
Z||000002||fieroshop||10-21-1999||09:40 AM||||I have 2.8 engines redone a few times and am getting ready to send another one out to be punched out. These engeines are much hotter than the stock by quite a bit. The heads and block are shaved for higher compression and the valves are ground to a different angle, the cam is also reground and we put roller rockers in the engine. This brings the hp up around 200 hp. But being a shop but not a machinest I leave the grinding work up to the experts||||reg||7
Z||000003||BOILERMAKER||10-21-1999||10:06 AM||||I'm not planning on doing the grounding myself.  I am just wanting to know what to tell the shop to do.  Considering that I have never done this I am looking for some pointers.  I figured that since I have a complete engine down to the block I figured that I could take in specific parts and get them done.  For exampler, boring the cylinders.  That's something that I can do now and need some info.<P>As far as getting together and swapping notes.  I am definitely game.  Anytime anybody has any info, drop me a line.<P>michael.hornsby@jci.com||||reg||7
Z||000004||Carrolles||10-21-1999||10:09 AM||||Phil Huff told me that the block can be bored out to .040 without problems.||||reg||1
Z||000005||RICK||10-21-1999||12:01 PM||||Take a look at this page, it might help <A HREF="http://hometown.aol.com/gpxss/rebuild/rebuild.html" TARGET=_blank>http://hometown.aol.com/gpxss/rebuild/rebuild.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||10-21-1999||01:01 PM||||The standard 2.8/3.1 overbore size is 0.030" over, but you can go 0.040" over. If I were doing it, I'd go 0.030" for a margin of safety. Most good machine shops want you to bring in the new set of pitons too, so they can bore and hone the cylinders to perfectly match the set of pistons you'll be using. So, the first step is to locate and purchase the pistons. Remember, if you are stroking it, you must buy 3.1 oversized pistons to work with the 3.1 crank.||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||10-21-1999||01:07 PM||||Oh yeah, when the block goes in to the shop, make sure the main caps are on it. Also, have the shop magnaflux (to check for cracks) and clean the block, then install new cam bearings at the same time as you get it bored out. They probably should check the deck for warpage too and you may need to mill the deck so the heads gaskets will seal good.||||reg||1
